= Provenance — Technical =

== PROV core model ==
W3C PROV represents provenance through entities, activities, and agents.

Conceptually:

<pre>
Entity --wasGeneratedBy--> Activity
Activity --used--> Entity
Entity --wasAttributedTo--> Agent
Activity --wasAssociatedWith--> Agent
Entity --wasDerivedFrom--> Entity
</pre>

The model supports bundles, collections, derivations, alternate/specialization relations, and time constraints.

== Provenance assertions ==
A provenance graph is not the past itself; it is a structured set of assertions about the past.

Validation therefore has two levels:
# structural validity of the provenance record;
# truth/adequacy of the assertions encoded in it.

W3C PROV-Constraints specifies consistency/inference constraints for PROV instances, not empirical truth of the recorded history.

== Lineage graphs ==
Data lineage can be modeled as a directed graph:

<pre>
source entities → transformations/activities → derived entities
</pre>

This supports:
* impact analysis;
* dependency tracing;
* reproducibility;
* audit;
* rollback/debugging;
* evidence-dependence analysis.

Cycles, aggregation, external inputs, and mutable resources require careful semantics.

== Provenance granularity ==
A provenance system must choose what events and objects are represented.

Too little granularity hides important transformations.

Too much granularity creates unusable records.

Useful provenance usually reflects the decisions users need to audit or reproduce.

== Trust and cryptographic integrity ==
Hashes, signatures, append-only logs, and attestations can make provenance tampering harder or establish authorship/integrity.

They cannot establish that the recorded event actually happened as described unless the recording process itself is trustworthy.

Cryptographic provenance therefore secures statements about lineage; it does not automatically validate their semantic truth.

== Provenance of provenance ==
Provenance records can themselves have provenance.

W3C PROV supports bundles and named provenance descriptions so systems can record who asserted a lineage and how that lineage record was produced.

